Yeah, I think that the way 3rd edition D&D handles it is effective:
give Rangers a +1 to hit and damage (with ranged weapons, this only
applies within I believe 30 feet) and various skill checks against the
favored enemy. You wouldn't need to make it selectable at game start,
and you wouldn't need to make it increase with level as it does in
D&D- because in D&D, frankly, characters gain a whole lot of abilities
that would probably be very unbalancing in Nethack. A class of
monsters would probably be best, and unless you're chaotic you
shouldn't ever have your own race as a member of the class. Thus a
Ranger might start out with bonuses against all d, which would
primarily be useful in the early to mid game (and might be very useful
during that period); or he or she might start out with an enemy of all
&, which wouldn't be terribly helpful for early-game survivability but
would be a nice bonus later on.
You could, of course, always balance this by giving corresponding
downsides: as you said, the favored enemy could always be hostile, or
they could be untamable. You could give them a +1 to hit and to damage
against the PC, but that would sort of go against the idea of the
Ranger's favored enemy (ie, that they have specialized training vs. a
specific monster type).

I don't know about the "racial ally" idea. That one just sounds a
little weird, and possibly game-breaking if randomly determined. What
luck, to have a racial ally of L, even if all it meant was that they
wouldn't attack you!

